[R0421] year=2026 | venue=World Wide Web | type=Article
TITLE: ELISAR: A Multi-Agent cybersecurity framework integrating retrieval-augmented generation for Blue, Red, and GRC operations
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cybersecurity; Generative AI; Governance; Large Language Models; Risk Assessment; Threat Mitigation; Autonomous agents; Compliant mechanisms; Computer system firewalls; Cybersecurity; Data privacy; Decision making; Intelligent agents; ISO Standards; Knowledge based systems; Multi agent systems; Regulatory compliance; Risk management; Agentic AI; Cyber security; Generative AI; Governance; Governance risks; Language model; Large language model; Risk and compliances; Risks assessments; Threats mitigations; Risk assessment
ABSTRACT: A cybersecurity framework, ELISAR, has been developed to address the evolving challenges of modern cybersecurity operations, including governance, risk management, and threat mitigation. Unlike conventional AI-driven models, ELISAR is designed as a collaborative multi-agent system, in which specialized agents are deployed to operate autonomously while interacting within a modular and scalable architecture. Multiple domain-specific agents are orchestrated by the ELISAR Engine, with each agent being optimized for distinct cybersecurity tasks. For instance, ELISAR for GRC (Governance, Risk, and Compliance) is supported by a vector-based knowledge retrieval mechanism trained on regulatory standards such as NIS2, ISO 27001, and ISO 42001, enabling compliance-driven decision-making. For penetration testing, a RAG enhanced knowledge base is utilized, curated from security assessment datasets to support proactive vulnerability exploration. The defensive suite, Blue ELISAR, is composed of intelligent sub-agents, including ELISAR Honeypots for deception, ELISAR Next-Gen Firewall for real-time detection, and ELISAR Smart DLP for adaptive data protection. Through the integration of autonomous reasoning, contextual knowledge retrieval, and real-time adaptability, ELISAR is presented as an interpretable and domain-specialized cybersecurity assistant, in contrast to monolithic LLM-based solutions. This extended version of the manuscript includes additional system design details, formal modeling, and an expanded experimental evaluation across defensive, offensive, and compliance cybersecurity domains. Improvements in accuracy, context relevance, and result stability are observed when agentic AI is combined with retrieval-based methods, while latency remains comparable across tasks. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2025.

[R0422] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of 3rd International Conference on Machine Intelligence and Digital Applications, MIDA 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: A Review of Large Language Models for Cybersecurity Defense: Core Capabilities, Application Practices, and Challenges
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity Defense; Defense Lifecycle; Intrusion Detection; Large Language Models; LLM Security; Threat Intelligence; Vulnerability Detection; Artificial intelligence; Codes (symbols); Cybersecurity; Digital forensics; Electronic crime countermeasures; Intelligent agents; Life cycle; Malware; Network security; Supply chains; Cyber security; Cybersecurity defense; Defense lifecycle; Intrusion-Detection; Language model; Large language model; Large language model security; Model security; Threat intelligence; Vulnerability detection; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: The rapid evolution of cyber threats - characterized by advanced persistent threats (APTs), software supply chain attacks, and AI-driven adversarial payloads - has exposed fundamental limitations in traditional rule-based and signature-based defense mechanisms. Large Language Models (LLMs), with core capabilities spanning natural language understanding, code engineering, logical reasoning, and autonomous decision-making, offer a transformative paradigm for cybersecurity defense. This paper presents a systematic review of LLM applications across the entire cybersecurity defense lifecycle. The review begins with an analysis of the core capabilities and key enabling technologies of LLMs - including prompt engineering, domain fine-tuning, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and agent frameworks - that underpin their adaptation to security tasks. Application practices are then examined across four critical stages: pre-attack prevention and secure development (covering vulnerability detection, secure code generation, supply chain security, and malware/phishing detection), during-attack detection and threat awareness (including network intrusion detection, log analysis, and threat intelligence automation), post-attack response and disposition (encompassing digital forensics and automated incident response),. The paper further dissects the security risks inherent to LLMs themselves - such as prompt injection, jailbreaking, data poisoning, and model stealing attacks - along with corresponding defense technologies. Finally, key challenges are identified, including hallucination, real-time performance bottlenecks, domain knowledge scarcity, data silos, interpretability deficits, and compliance risks, with future research directions proposed toward building more robust, trustworthy, and human-centered intelligent defense systems. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

[R0423] year=2026 | venue=4th Cognitive Models and Artificial Intelligence Conference, AICCONF 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Stateful LLM Agents for Autonomous Capture-the-Flag Solving
KEYWORDS: Autonomous agents; Capture-the-Flag (CTF); Large language models (LLMs); Penetration testing; Application programming interfaces (API); Case based reasoning; Cryptography; Intelligent agents; Interface states; Multi agent systems; Network security; Capture the flag; Capture-the-flag; End to end; Language model; Large language model; Model agents; Multisteps; Penetration testing; Remote shell; Work-flows; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: We study end-to-end autonomy in tool-using large language model (LLM) agents for offensive-security under a strictly stateful execution contract. We implement an explicit state-machine-orchestrated agent that (i) maintains a persistent remote shell session, (ii) enforces a structured action interface that produces exactly one command or an explicit credential-extraction event, and (iii) logs and checkpoints the intermediate state for auditing and reproducibility. We evaluate the same workflow across API-based LLM backbones on two OverTheWire benchmarks with complementary profiles: Bandit (multi-step remote shell interaction) and Krypton (cryptanalytic reasoning). On Bandit, standalone (per-level) success substantially exceeds sequential (chained) success, revealing that long-horizon statefulness and closed-loop robustness are the primary bottlenecks. Among the mini models, sequential completion increases monotonically, while standalone completion reach more complex levels. Instantiating our stateful harness with GPT-4o matches a prior stateless GPT-4o standalone baseline (20/33), but exposes a large sequential drop (16/33) that is invisible without chaining. In contrast, Krypton is dominated by reasoning- and interface-limited failures: models reliably solve early substitution/encoding levels but fail on higher levels requiring multi-step statistical cryptanalysis and, in several cases, interactive stdin-driven workflows not supported by a single-command execution interface. Together, these results motivate sequential evaluation as a necessary metric for autonomous penetration-testing pipelines and clarify where current agentic systems fail: interaction robustness in long horizons versus procedural competence in multi-step reasoning. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0424] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 23rd International Joint Conference on Computer Science and Software Engineering, JCSSE 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SHARKAPT: An Autonomous LLM-Orchestrated Penetration Testing Framework with MCP-Based Tool Integration
