ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is still highly dependent on trained professionals (who are required to manually coordinate heterogeneous security tools, multi-phase findings, and actionable reports) that is both time-consuming, expertise-oriented and inadequate to the speed of current threats. The framework described in this paper, SHARKAPT, is an autonomous AI-based penetration testing framework that uses the Model Context Protocol and large language model orchestration to conduct end-to-end automated security testing of both network and web application-related systems. SHARKAPT uses a multi-agent architecture based on LangGraph with five specialised agents: reconnaissance, network penetration testing, web application testing, exploitation as well as report generation and a decision engine that dynamically chooses 60+ real-world security tools. Google Gemini 3.1 Pro evaluated empirically against Metasploitable2 and OWASP Juice Shop has coverage of 71.7% of the Open Web Application Security Project Top 10 2021 challenges (9/10 categories), 79.1% Metasploitable2 known-vulnerability coverage, a 99.1% step success rate on the web session (111 steps), and a 94.5% step success rate on the network session (73 steps). All finding results are rated using a deterministic CVSS v3.0 calculator and stored in organised HTML reports containing per-finding evidence. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0425] year=2026 | venue=2026 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence Systems, AIS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic AI in Cybersecurity:Threat Modeling and Containment Strategies for Autonomous LLM Agents
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; AI Safety; Autonomous Agents; Containment Architecture; Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; Prompt Injection; Threat Modeling; Agents; Architecture; Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Memory architecture; Modeling languages; Pipelines; Agentic AI; AI safety; Attack vector; Containment architecture; Cyber security; Language model; Large language model; Model agents; Prompt injection; Threat modeling; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: The rapid proliferation of autonomous Large Language Model (LLM) agents has introduced a largely uncharacterized attack surface in production AI deployments. Unlike static model inference, agentic pipelines expose dynamic execution paths, persistent memory, and third-party tool integrations that adversaries can exploit through carefully crafted inputs. This paper presents a structured threat model for autonomous LLM agents operating in cybersecurity-sensitive environments. The research objective is to identify, formalize, and mitigate attack vectors unique to agentic LLM pipelines that existing security frameworks fail to address. We formalize five primary attack vectors: prompt injection chaining, tool misuse via intent manipulation, goal drift through context poisoning, memory persistence exploitation, and cross-agent trust escalation. We provide empirical demonstrations using three publicly available agentic frameworks - AutoGPT, LangChain, and CrewAI - and propose CyberShield-A, a three-layer containment architecture comprising input sanitization, execution sandboxing, and behavioral auditing. Evaluation across 47 red-team scenarios demonstrates that CyberShield-A reduces successful attack completion rates by 73.4% while preserving 91.2% task utility. A full 23 ablation study confirms that all three layers contribute independently significant protection (p<0.001 for each). These findings establish a foundational taxonomy and deployable architecture for securing the next generation of autonomous AI systems. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0426] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=Article
TITLE: Secrets That Survive Everything: Runtime Credential Exposure in Production Web Applications
KEYWORDS: API key exposure; APIM; Azure AD; credential exposure; CryptoJS; JavaScript security; LLM-assisted ground truth; McNemar test; penetration testing; Runtime secret detection; shift-left security; shift-right gap; UpSet plot; web application security; webpack; Application programming interfaces (API); Codes (symbols); Cryptography; Extraction; Network security; Security systems; API key exposure; APIM; Azure AD; Credential exposure; Cryptojs; Ground truth; Javascript; Javascript security; Key exposure; LLM-assisted ground truth; McNemar's tests; Penetration testing; Runtime secret detection; Runtimes; Shift-leave security; Shift-right gap; Upset plot; Web application security; Webpack; High level languages
ABSTRACT: Pre-deployment secret scanning operates only on source code, never on what a production application serves. We document two exploitation chains in which Azure AD client credentials and APIM subscription keys from production JavaScript bundles enabled account takeover and mass data exposure. An authorized engagement covered approximately 2,000 enterprise web assets in one organization; 113 (5.65%) served live credentials. To quantify the shift-right gap, we built an independent Ground Truth (GT-194) of 194 secret-grade credentials through Claude Opus 4.7 extraction and manual analyst review, with the 247 LLM-extracted candidates independently validated by GPT-5.5 (Brennan-Prediger κ = 0.676). The principal finding is structural: 13.9% of GT-194 (27 of 194) is surfaced only by manual analysis and recovered by none of the nine evaluated production scanners, a tool-agnostic blind spot the ground-truth model also misses. CryptoJS encrypted configuration separately defeats every static scanner: the credential exists only after decryption with a co-located key, reached only by runtime-aware detection. Combined coverage plateaus at 86.1%. Among the nine scanners, the best static scanner recovers 36.6% and the best runtime-aware scanner 77.8% (F1 = 0.818, McNemar p < 0.001); the ground-truth model is reported separately as a reference comparator, not an evaluated detector. On 63 of 86 secret-exposed applications (73.3%), the full Azure AD token-mint chain is co-located in one bundle, reachable from browser code. We characterize five paths by which credentials reach production undetected and present a layered runtime detection methodology and remediation framework. Recall is scoped to a single-organization Azure-heavy corpus. © 2013 IEEE.

[R0427] year=2025 | venue=Applied Sciences (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: LogRESP-Agent: A Recursive AI Framework for Context-Aware Log Anomaly Detection and TTP Analysis
