ABSTRACT: In the increasingly challenging landscape of cyberspace, penetration testing has garnered significant attention as a means of maintaining cybersecurity. The automation and intelligent enhancement of penetration testing have become key areas of current research. Particularly, the focus of automated penetration testing research lies in penetration path planning and penetration decision generation. This review provides a comprehensive overview of the methodologies related to automated penetration testing, with an emphasis on the development processes and current research status of penetration path planning and penetration decision generation. It also summarizes the current limitations and challenges in this field and offers perspectives on future development. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2025.

[R0432] year=2024 | venue=Proceedings - 2024 IEEE 6th International Conference on Trust, Privacy and Security in Intelligent Systems, and Applications, TPS-ISA 2024 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Forensic Analysis of Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks on LLM Agents
KEYWORDS: agent; digital forensics; indirect prompt injection; LLM; Autonomous agents; Benchmarking; Computer forensics; Crime; Forensic engineering; Modeling languages; Network security; Agent execution; Data volume; Digital forensic analysis; Digital investigation; Execution logs; Forensic analysis; Indirect prompt injection; Language model; Large language model; Model agents; Electronic crime countermeasures
ABSTRACT: Large language model (LLM) agents are vulnerable to a range of evolving attacks including Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI). Digital investigations involving IPI attacks on LLM agents are challenging due to growing data volumes and agent flow complexity. We introduce a novel approach for forensic analysis of LLM agent execution logs to identify IPI attacks. We implement a custom digital forensic analysis framework featuring a realistic agentic task benchmark to generate agent logs, for subsequent analysis using 12 state-of-the-art LLMs. Our preliminary results show promising potential for malicious trail detection, with varying performance across domain-specific test suites. We discuss key findings, limitations, and future work directions to support the empirical evaluation of LLMs for digital forensic applications. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0433] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications, ICCSDFAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AI-Driven Penetration Testing: A Survey of Autonomous Agents, LLM-Based Approaches, and Future Directions
KEYWORDS: AI red teaming; autonomous agents; cybersecurity automation; large language models; LLM security; penetration testing; reinforcement learning; vulnerability discovery; Autonomous agents; Benchmarking; Computation theory; Computer architecture; Formal verification; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Network security; AI red teaming; Cyber security; Cybersecurity automation; Language model; Large language model; Large language model security; Model security; Penetration testing; Red teaming; Reinforcement learnings; Vulnerability discovery; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: Adversarial pressure on digital infrastructure is accelerating faster than the supply of skilled penetration testers, exposing a structural gap that manual testing alone cannot close. This survey examines the emerging body of work on AI-driven penetration testing, covering reinforcement learning (RL) agents, large language model (LLM)-based systems, machine learning-assisted vulnerability discovery, and hybrid architectures that combine multiple paradigms. We analyze seven representative systems and approaches - including PentestGPT, HackingBuddyGPT, AutoAttacker, CyberBattleSim, and IAPTS - characterizing each by demonstrated capability, evaluation environment, and measurable limitation. Our three primary contributions are: (1) a structured taxonomy of AI-driven penetration testing approaches organized around agent architecture and reasoning strategy; (2) a comparative analysis table that maps each system to standardized dimensions, enabling direct cross-system comparison; and (3) an identification of four cross-cutting open problems - hallucination in exploit generation, evaluation benchmark fragmentation, the simulation-to-reality transfer gap, and the regulatory vacuum around autonomous offensive tooling. We close by proposing directions for future work including agentic long-horizon planning, formal exploit verification, and federated security learning. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0434] year=2026 | venue=Information Fusion | type=Article
TITLE: PTFusion: LLM-driven context-aware knowledge fusion for web penetration testing
KEYWORDS: Chain-of-Thought; Dynamic knowledge graphs; Large language models; Multi-agent systems; Web penetration testing; Autonomous agents; Chains; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Knowledge management; Network architecture; Chain-of-thought; Context-Aware; Dynamic knowledge graph; Knowledge fusion; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Penetration testing tools; Web penetration testing; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: This paper presents PTFusion, an LLM-driven web penetration testing framework that addresses inefficient task guidance and imprecise command execution challenges in web penetration testing. Employing a semi-decentralized multi-agent collaborative architecture, PTFusion maintains strategic coherence while enabling autonomous tactical execution, and uses the Model Context Protocol to more conveniently call different types of penetration testing tools. To effectively guide task execution, the PTFusion designs a context-aware knowledge fusion mechanism to plan tasks based on the dynamic knowledge graph and executed actions, and uses the preference-based chain-of-thought prompting to address the issue of redundant and difficult to align outputs from different types of penetration testing tools. Compared to methods like PentestGPT, PTFusion demonstrates significantl superior performance in both task completion effectiveness and stability. The context-aware knowledge fusion mechanism enables PTFusion to conduct more precise strategic planning and execute penetration testing commands with greater accuracy, ensuring reliable completion of web penetration testing tasks across various scenarios. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.

[R0435] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Big Data, BigData | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Design and Implementation of a Multi-Agent Threat Intelligence Assistant Based on Generative AI
KEYWORDS: Cyber Threat Intelligence; Cybersecurity; Generative AI; LLM; Computation theory; Cybersecurity; Knowledge based systems; Open systems; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Daily activity; Design and implementations; Generative AI; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Open source intelligence; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Cyber threat intelligence programs are designed to support the daily activities of cybersecurity analysts. However, most of the existing tools are unable to provide accurate information. To fill this gap and provide analysts with verified data, we propose an interactive assistant leveraging generative AI techniques and large language models (LLMs) to identify and categorize information related to threat actors based on open-source intelligence (OSINT). Users can interact with the assistant through a simple chat interface, asking questions in natural language. Based on the user's question, the assistant (i) queries a large knowledge base containing information about numerous threat actors collected from multiple OSINT sources and (ii) processes the response to make it comprehensible to the end user. The assistant, enabled with LLM capabilities, can also be used to generate summaries from cybersecurity research reports or extract specific information from them. A preliminary evaluation, conducted with experts in the field, demonstrates the effectiveness of the proposed assistant in supporting the daily activities of cyber threat analysts. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0436] year=2025 | venue=Electronics (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: Cybersecurity Applications of Near-Term Large Language Models
