TITLE: A Qualitative Study on Using ChatGPT for Software Security: Perception vs. Practicality
KEYWORDS: large language model; security; software vulnerability; Semantics; Software testing; Knowledge intensive tasks; Language model; Large language model; Qualitative study; Security; Semantic accuracy; Semantics understanding; Software security; Software vulnerabilities; Vulnerability detection; Information leakage
ABSTRACT: Artificial Intelligence (AI) advancements have enabled the development of Large Language Models (LLMs) that can perform a variety of tasks with remarkable semantic understanding and accuracy. ChatGPT is one such LLM that has gained significant attention due to its impressive capabilities for assisting in various knowledge-intensive tasks. Due to the knowledge-intensive nature of engineering secure software, Chat-GPT's assistance is expected to be explored for security-related tasks during the development/evolution of software. To gain an understanding of the potential of ChatGPT as an emerging technology for supporting software security, we adopted a twofold approach. Initially, we performed an empirical study to analyse the perceptions of those who had explored the use of ChatGPT for security tasks and shared their views on Twitter. It was determined that security practitioners view ChatGPT as beneficial for various software security tasks, including vulnerability detection, information retrieval, and penetration testing. Secondly, we designed an experiment aimed at investigating the practicality of this technology when deployed as an oracle in real-world settings. In particular, we focused on vulnerability detection and qualitatively examined ChatGPT outputs for given prompts within this prominent software security task. Based on our analysis, responses from ChatGPT in this task are largely filled with generic security information and may not be appropriate for industry use. To prevent data leakage, we performed this analysis on a vulnerability dataset compiled after the OpenAI data cut-off date from real-world projects covering 40 distinct vulnerability types and 12 programming languages. We assert that the findings from this study would contribute to future research aimed at developing and evaluating LLMs dedicated to software security. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0438] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications, TrustCom | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AutoPentestAL: An automated penetration testing framework with active learning capabilities
KEYWORDS: and Automatic Knowledge Learning; Large Language Model; Multi-agent; Penetration Testing; Automation; Benchmarking; Learning systems; Problem solving; And automatic knowledge learning; Knowledge learning; Language model; Large language model; Model-based penetration testing; Multi agent; Penetration testing; Testing framework; Tool usages; Work-flows; Ability testing
ABSTRACT: The rapid development of large language models (LLMs) provides new possibilities for intelligent penetration testing. However, existing LLM-based penetration testing still face the following challenges. (1) LLMs lack the memory retention ability of long-term conversations, and when dealing with multistep penetration testing tasks, they lose the information given above, leading to the generation error strategy.(2) The existing LLM-based penetration testing framework does not have a clear division of tasks for agents, making it difficult to establish a complete workflow, resulting in poor completion of penetration testing tasks. (3) There is a large amount of code text in exploit tools, and most of these code texts do not contain tool usage information, making it difficult for traditional RAG methods to search for tool usage methods in chunksTo solve the above problems, we propose an LLM-based automated penetration testing framework AutoPentestAL. Firstly, the task linked list is designed to decompose complex problems into simple problems, reducing the length of steps required to solve the problem and avoiding the problem of missing information in the process of solving the problem of LLM. Secondly, the penetration testing tasks are broken down in detail, and the agents responsible for each task are carefully designed to establish a complete automated penetration testing workflow. Finally, the tool actively learns the method ToolAL, learns the parameter configuration and other information in the specific use of the exploit tool, and constructs an accurate vectorized index to improve the accuracy of LLM retrieval of tool usage methodsThe experimental results show that the completion rate of the penetration test of the AutoPentestAL framework designed in this paper is 86%, which is 72% higher than that of the benchmark method. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0439] year=2026 | venue=Cybersecurity | type=Article
TITLE: KGAgent4CTI: unlocking the power of LLM in threat intelligence
KEYWORDS: Attack knowledge graph construction; Cyber threat intelligence analysis; Large language models; Multi-agent; Retrieval-augmented generation; Behavioral research; Distributed computer systems; Graph theory; Intelligent agents; Knowledge graph; Network security; Parameterization; Attack knowledge graph construction; Cybe threat intelligence analyze; Cyber threats; Graph construction; Intelligence analysis; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Retrieval-augmented generation; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Cyber threat intelligence (CTI) serves as the cognitive hub for cybersecurity defense, deeply integrating the domain knowledge of security experts with the characteristics of attack behaviors. Constructing attack knowledge graphs from CTI provides critical support for attack chain reconstruction and defensive decision-making. However, traditional knowledge graph construction methods exhibit significant limitations when facing the domain-specific challenges of CTI: ambiguous entity boundaries leading to named entity recognition (NER) drift, the long-tail phenomenon causing the omission of low-frequency threat elements, and difficulties in modeling multi-hop contextual dependencies. Furthermore, the dynamic evolution of adversarial technique frameworks such as MITRE ATT&CK poses a severe version drift risk for traditional models that rely on parameterized knowledge storage. To address these challenges, this paper introduces KGAgent4CTI—a threat intelligence analysis framework based on large language models (LLMs). Its core breakthroughs are twofold: (1) It establishes a multi-agent collaborative architecture that, through a task decoupling mechanism, decomposes knowledge graph construction into five specialized modules, enabling a progressive cognitive enhancement for attack chain analysis. (2) It designs a dynamic knowledge adaptation engine that combines a hybrid retrieval-augmented generation strategy with version-aware indexing technology, overcoming the limitations of an LLM’s parameterized knowledge storage to achieve precise, zero-shot identification of attack techniques. Our experimental results indicate that, compared with state-of-the-art methods, KGAgent4CTI demonstrates significant improvements, achieving an attack technique identification precision of 92.3% while also reducing the need for manual intervention and computational resources. Furthermore, the knowledge graphs we construct directly enable downstream security tasks, such as attack scenario reconstruction and organizational attribution. © The Author(s) 2026.

[R0440] year=2026 | venue=Algorithms | type=Article
TITLE: AgentRed: Towards an Agent-Based Approach to Automated Network Attack Traffic Generation
KEYWORDS: agent; fine-tuning; GRPO; large language models; LoRA; network traffic generation; parameter-efficient fine-tuning; reinforcement learning; synthetic datasets; Autonomous agents; Complex networks; Computer crime; Internet protocols; Large datasets; Learning systems; Network architecture; Network security; Online systems; Search engines; Telecommunication traffic; Tuning; Fine tuning; GRPO; Language model; Large language model; Low-rank adaptation; Network traffic generation; Parameter-efficient fine-tuning; Reinforcement learnings; Synthetic datasets; Agents
ABSTRACT: Network security tools are indispensable in testing and evaluating the security of computer networks. Existing tools, such as Hping3, however, offer a limited set of options and attack-specific configurations, which restrict their use solely to well-known attack patterns. Although highly parameterizable libraries, such as Scapy, provide more options and scripting capabilities, they require extensive manual setup and often a steep learning curve. The development of powerful AI models, capitalizing on the transformer architecture, has enabled cybersecurity researchers to develop or incorporate these models into existing cyber-defense systems and red-team assessments. Prominent models such as NetGPT, TrafficFormer, and TrafficGPT can be effective, but require extensive computational resources for fine-tuning and a complex setup to adapt to proprietary networking environments and protocols. In this work, we propose AgentRed, a lightweight tool for generating network attack traffic with minimal human configuration and setup. Our tool integrates an AI agent and a large language model with fewer than a billion parameters into the network traffic generation process. Our method creates lightweight Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) adapters that can learn specific traffic patterns in a particular network environment. Our agent can autonomously train the LoRA adapters, search online documentation for attack patterns and parameters, and select appropriate adapters to generate network traffic specific to the user’s needs. It utilizes the LoRA adapters to create an intermediate traffic representation that can be parsed and executed by tools such as Scapy to generate malicious traffic in a virtualized test environment. We assess the performance of the proposed approach on six popular network attacks, including flooding attacks, Smurf, Ping-of-Death, and normal ICMP ping traffic. Our results validate the ability of the proposed tool to efficiently generate network packets with 97.9% accuracy using the LoRA adapters, compared to 95.4% accuracy using the base pre-trained Qwen3 0.6B model. When the AI agent performs online searches to enrich the LoRA adapters’ context during traffic generation, our method maintains an accuracy of 96.0% across all tested traffic patterns. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0441] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 2026 IEEE 12th Conference on Big Data Security on Cloud, BigDataSecurity 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: TRACE: Tamper-Resistant Accountability and Cryptographic Evidence for Multi-Agent AI Systems
KEYWORDS: accountability; AI security; cryptographic commitments; forensic attribution; multi-agent systems; provenance tracking; tamper-detection; Ciphertext; Computer architecture; Cryptography; Digital forensics; Forensic engineering; Forestry; Graphic methods; Intelligent agents; Network architecture; Network security; Accountability; AI security; AI systems; Cryptographic commitment; CryptoGraphics; Forensic attribution; Multi agent; Multiagent systems (MASs); Provenance tracking; Tamper detection; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Modern AI systems increasingly rely on composite architectures, chaining multiple models, retrievers, and agents to solve complex tasks. When these systems fail or are compromised in security-critical domains like healthcare, finance, or autonomous vehicles, determining which component is responsible and whether the audit trail has been tampered with is nearly impossible with existing tools. We introduce TRACE, a security-first framework that enables cryptographically-verified forensic reconstruction of decision provenance in multi-agent AI systems. Our contributions include: (1) a formal provenance graph representation capturing causal relationships across model pipelines with cryptographic commitments, (2) degradation-based attribution methods for identifying compromised or faulty components, (3) Merkle tree-based commitment protocols providing tamper-evident audit trails with provable security guarantees, and (4) temporal reconstruction methods enabling secure audits of historical decisions even after system updates. We validate our framework on a deployed RAG architecture using OpenAI GPT-4o-mini, Pinecone vector search, and Cohere reranking, with a corpus of 12,000 passages. Through controlled fault injection experiments on 80 test cases, we demonstrate 90% attribution accuracy in identifying faulty components with 10% runtime overhead, while our cryptographic protocols provide tamper-detection with 2-256 forgery probability. This framework addresses critical security gaps in AI system accountability, providing both forensic attribution capabilities and cryptographic guarantees essential for deploying multi-agent AI systems in security-sensitive environments. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0442] year=2025 | venue=ACM SIGCITE 2025 - Proceedings of the 26th ACM Annual Conference on Cybersecurity and Information Technology Education | type=Conference paper
TITLE: BAIT: A Social Engineering Practice Platform
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity education; LLM; Social engineering; Behavioral research; Cybersecurity; Education computing; Engineering education; Human engineering; Iterative methods; Personnel training; Social sciences computing; Cyber security; Cyber-security educations; Design-based research; Engineering practices; LLM; Penetration test; Practice platforms; Sensitive informations; Social engineering; Unauthorized access; Students
ABSTRACT: Social engineering, defined as manipulative efforts to trick individuals into revealing sensitive information or granting unauthorized access, remains among organizations' most significant vulnerabilities. Increasingly, cybersecurity companies are asked to perform social engineering penetration tests to train employees to not fall for common deception techniques. This design-based research paper introduces the Behavioral Attack & Influence Training (BAIT) platform built to give cybersecurity students hands-on experience performing SE campaigns in a fictional, yet authentic, penetration testing context. We describe the core goals, functionality, technical architecture, AI implementation, and lessons learned from the iterative development and testing of BAIT with 64 students, as well as future directions for projects designed to give students social engineering practice in ethical ways. © 2025 Copyright is held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.

[R0443] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE International Conference for Convergence in Computing Technology, I3CTCON 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Generative AI for Synthetic Cyber Threat Simulation and Defense Training
KEYWORDS: Adversarial Machine Learning; Autonomous Cyber Defence; Cyber Defence Training; Federated Learning; Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI); Large Language Models; Privacy-Preserving Security; Reinforcement Learning; Synthetic Cyber Threat Simulation; Threat Intelligence; Behavioral research; Cybersecurity; Differential privacy; Learning systems; Malware; Network security; Public works; Security systems; Sensitive data; Autonomous cybe defense; Cybe defense training; Cyber threats; Cyber-defense; Generative artificial intelligence; Language model; Large language model; Machine-learning; Privacy preserving; Privacy-preserving security; Reinforcement learnings; Synthetic cybe threat simulation; Threat intelligence; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: The growing complexity, magnitude, and occurrence of cyberattacks have revealed critical shortcomings in the conventional approaches to cybersecurity training which are typically based on fixed datasets and pre-determined attacks. Such strategies are not sufficient to equip security systems and practitioners with zero-day, adaptive, and emerging threats. To overcome this difficulty, this paper suggests an artificial intelligence (GenAI)-based model of synthetic cyber threat simulation and defence training, which would allow forming realistic and diverse, as well as dynamically changing environments of attacks without exposing sensitive organizational data. The proposed framework uses the developed large language models based on transformers, generative adversarial networks, and diffusion models to generate high-fidelity threat artifacts, including phishing schemes, patterns of malware behaviour, network intrusion evidence, and social engineering tactics. The system can optimise attack simulation through the integration of the reinforcement learning-inspired adversarial agents to simulate behaviour of the real-world threat actors in order to enhance the resilience and versatility of defensive mechanisms. Moreover, the architecture is designed with privacy-protective features like federated learning and differential privacy to enable sharing of threat intelligence in collaboration, without having to disclose the data. We perform automated feedback on defence performance on the basis of major cybersecurity indicators, such as detection rates, response time, resilience, and false-positive rates, in addition to adaptive training of security analysts and AI-based defence systems. Through experimentation, it is established that the suggested GenAI-based simulation environment will significantly increase the preparedness to threats, decrease training expenses, and better detectives than traditional rule-based cyber range models. The framework is scalable and can be used to deploy across cloud-edge environments, thus it can be applied to enterprise security operations centres, critical infrastructure protection, and autonomous cyber protection ecosystems. This publication underscores the radical nature of generative AI to redefine cybersecurity as a reactive protection to proactive, intelligence-driven resilience in cybersecurity, thus providing the basis of the next-generation cyber defence training platforms. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0444] year=2024 | venue=Proceedings - IEEE Military Communications Conference MILCOM | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Exploring the Potential of Large Language Models for Red Teaming in Military Coalition Networks
KEYWORDS: Autonomous offensive agents; Coalition networks; Large Language Models; Penetration testing; Digital elevation model; Intelligent systems; Autonomous offensive agent; Coalition networks; Language model; Large language model; Military coalition network; Penetration test; Penetration testing; Performance; Red teaming; Test scripts; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: This paper reports on an ongoing investigation comparing the performance of large language models (LLMs) in generating penetration test scripts for realistic red agents. The goal is to develop human-level adversaries (red agents) in an automated cyber operations gym environment instrumented to train a team of blue agents. Our methodology defines five approaches for structuring the prompts used to generate Metasploit scripts to exploit vulnerabilities described in Common Vulnerabilities Exposures (CVEs). These approaches have been tested using three LLMs, namely GPT-4o, WhiteRabbitNeo, and Mistral-7b. GPT-4o is used as the baseline for our comparison study. The results suggest that GPT-4o outperforms the other LLMs in all experiments. However, the results also suggest that Mistral-7b can be fine-tuned to achieve acceptable performance while consuming much less computational and memory resources during execution due to a smaller number of parameters: 7 billion parameters for Mistral-7b versus 1.76 trillion for GPT-4o. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0445] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes of the Institute for Computer Sciences, Social-Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering, LNICST | type=Conference paper
TITLE: APT-Agent: A Training-Free, Unsupervised, LLM-Based Multi-agent Framework for Interpretable Advanced Persistent Threat Detection
KEYWORDS: Advanced Persistent Threat; Large Language Model; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Unsupervised Approach; Computational efficiency; Deep learning; Intelligent agents; Labeled data; Large datasets; Learning systems; Natural language processing systems; Advanced persistent threat; Cyber security; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent framework; Retrieval-augmented generation; Targeting precision; Threat agents; Threat detection; Unsupervised approaches; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs)—noted for their stealth, targeting precision, and destructive impact—have become a major challenge in cybersecurity. With the progress of deep learning, deep-learning-based APT detection has emerged as the mainstream research direction. Nevertheless, current methods still face three key limitations: (1) dependence on large, labeled datasets containing attack samples and extensive domain knowledge; (2) prohibitive computational costs during training and inference, making real-world deployment difficult; and (3) a narrow focus on merely determining whether an attack exists, lacking interpretability and support for follow-up analysis. To overcome these issues, we introduce APT-Agent, the first training-free, unsupervised LLM-based multi-agent framework dedicated to APT detection. It coordinates three agents: (1) a Dispatcher Agent that decomposes natural-language requests into subtasks, dispatches them for execution, and aggregates the results into a unified response; (2) a Detection Agent that fuses LLM reasoning with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for efficient and accurate log detection; and (3) an Interpreter Agent that combines long-term memory with external tools to generate trustworthy explanations and mitigation advice. Evaluation results show that APT-Agent achieves outstanding detection performance across ten attack scenarios while incurring dramatically lower computational overhead than state-of-the-art APT detection methods. © ICST Institute for Computer Sciences, Social Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering 2026.

[R0446] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Network | type=Article
TITLE: Generative AI-Enhanced Neuro-Symbolic Quantum Architectures for Secure Communications and Networking
KEYWORDS: Generative AI; Intelligent networks; Multi-agents; Neuro-symbolic; Quantum AI; Secure communications; Artificial intelligence; Cryptography; Intelligent agents; Intelligent networks; Learning systems; Network architecture; Network security; Pattern recognition; Quantum communication; Security systems; Communications and networkings; Generative AI; Multi agent; Neuro-symbolic; Quantum AI; Quantum architecture; Quantum Computing; Rapid convergence; Real- time; Symbolic reasoning; Secure communication
ABSTRACT: The rapid convergence of generative AI (GAI), neuro-symbolic reasoning, and quantum computing has redefined secure communication and networking. Owing to the massive amounts of data, the need for extensive computing power, and the necessity to find and mitigate threats in real-time, traditional security measures cannot keep up with changing cyber threats as the communication infrastructure becomes more complicated. Therefore, this paper investigates the potential of a GAI-enhanced neuro-symbolic quantum framework for building security systems that are strong, scalable, and self-sufficient. This method improves threat detection, encryption, and real-time adversarial defense by combining symbolic reasoning for logic, deep learning for pattern recognition, and quantum intelligence to accelerate computations. This work has made progress in creating the next generation of secure networks that can automatically and adaptively make security decisions in real-time, providing robust protection in communication environments that change over time. © 1986-2012 IEEE.

[R0447] year=2026 | venue=Future Generation Computer Systems | type=Article
TITLE: CyberRAG: An agentic RAG cyber attack classification and reporting tool
KEYWORDS: Agentic retrieval-augmented generation; Cyber threat detection; Fine-tuned security classifiers; Intrusion detection systems; Large language models; Adaptive control systems; Autonomous agents; Classification (of information); Crime; Information retrieval; Intrusion detection; Knowledge based systems; Learning systems; Machine learning; Malware; Natural language processing systems; Network intrusion; Network security; Semantics; Agentic retrieval-augmented generation; Cybe threat detection; Cyber threats; Cyber-attacks; False positive; Fine-tuned security classifier; Intrusion Detection Systems; Language model; Large language model; Threat detection; Computational methods
ABSTRACT: Intrusion Detection and Prevention Systems (IDS/IPS) in large enterprises can generate hundreds of thousands of alerts per hour, overwhelming analysts with logs requiring rapidly evolving expertise. Conventional machine-learning detectors reduce alert volume but still yield many false positives, while standard Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines often retrieve irrelevant context and fail to justify predictions. We present CyberRAG, a modular agent-based RAG framework that delivers real-time classification, explanation, and structured reporting for cyber-attacks. A central LLM agent orchestrates: (i) fine-tuned classifiers specialized by attack family; (ii) tool adapters for enrichment and alerting; and (iii) an iterative retrieval-and-reason loop that queries a domain-specific knowledge base until evidence is relevant and self-consistent. Unlike traditional RAG, CyberRAG adopts an agentic design that enables dynamic control flow and adaptive reasoning. This architecture autonomously refines threat labels and natural-language justifications, reducing false positives and enhancing interpretability. It is also extensible: new attack types can be supported by adding classifiers without retraining the core agent. CyberRAG was evaluated on SQL Injection, XSS, and SSTI, achieving over 94 % accuracy per class and a final classification accuracy of 94.92 % through semantic orchestration. Generated explanations reached 0.94 in BERTScore and 4.9/5 in GPT-4-based expert evaluation, with robustness preserved against adversarial and unseen payloads. These results show that agentic, specialist-oriented RAG can combine high detection accuracy with trustworthy, SOC-ready prose, offering a flexible path toward partially automated cyber-defense workflows. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

[R0448] year=2025 | venue=UMAP 2025 - Adjunct Proceedings of the 33rd ACM Conference on User Modeling, Adaptation and Personalization | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Towards Automated Penetration Testing: Introducing LLM Benchmark, Analysis, and Improvements
KEYWORDS: Benchmarking; Capture the Flag; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Cybersecurity; Human engineering; Network security; Benchmark analysis; Capture the flag; Cyber security; End to end; Language model; Large language model; Model-based penetration testing; Penetration testing; Security context; State of the art; Benchmarking
ABSTRACT: Hacking poses a significant threat to cybersecurity, inflicting billions of dollars in damages annually. To mitigate these risks, ethical hacking, or penetration testing, is employed to identify vulnerabilities in systems and networks. Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have shown potential across various domains, including cybersecurity. However, there is currently no comprehensive, open, end-To-end penetration testing benchmark to drive progress and evaluate the capabilities of these models in security contexts. This paper introduces a novel open benchmark1 for LLM-based penetration testing, addressing this critical gap. We first evaluate the performance of LLMs, including GPT-4o and LLama 3.1-405B, using the state-of-The-Art PentestGPT tool. Our findings reveal that while LLama 3.1 demonstrates an edge over GPT-4o, both models currently fall short of performing end-To-end penetration testing even with some minimal human assistance. Next, we advance the state-of-The-Art and present ablation studies that provide insights into improving the PentestGPT tool2. Our research illuminates the challenges LLMs face in each aspect of Pentesting, e.g. enumeration, exploitation, and privilege escalation. This work contributes to the growing body of knowledge on AI-Assisted cybersecurity and lays the foundation for future research in automated penetration testing using large language models. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).


